{"kind": "report", "step": 17, "ts": "2026-11-19T01:19:55.089325+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "gw.lan", "component": "service_config", "cause": "gw.lan's unbound applies its DNS-rebinding protection (private-address: 10.0.0.0/8) to answers forwarded from dns1.lan for the .lan zone, so it returns an empty NOERROR answer for nas.lan and other local names; whenever a client query lands on gw (or falls through after dns1's per-/24 ratelimit drops queries during traffic bursts), getaddrinfo sees a name with no address and reports ENOTFOUND until the next retry succeeds.", "evidence": ["dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan on mon.lan: NOERROR, ANSWER: nas.lan 10 IN A 10.20.0.50 \u2014 dns1 has the record (short 10s TTL)", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan on mon.lan: NOERROR but ANSWER: 0, AUTHORITY: 0 \u2014 gw consistently returns an empty answer for the same name; host nas.lan 10.20.0.1 confirms 'no address' while host nas.lan 10.20.0.3 returns 10.20.0.50", "drill nas.lan @127.0.0.1 on gw itself also returns an empty answer, so this is not a network path issue \u2014 gw's own resolver cannot present the forwarded answer", "cat /var/unbound/unbound.conf on gw: 'private-address: 10.0.0.0/8' with comment 'DNS rebinding protection: drop answers for public names that point at private addresses', plus forward-zone lan. -> 10.20.0.3; the filter strips the legitimate local answers because there is no private-domain exemption for lan.", "journalctl -u primary-dns on dns1.lan: 'ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping 23/31/8 queries' at 02:14, 09:41 and 13:02 (config.yaml sets ratelimit: 20 per /24)", "journalctl on mon.lan: monitor alerts '[media] DOWN ... getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND media.lan' at 09:41-09:42 and '[nas] WARN getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND nas.lan' at 13:02 \u2014 timestamps match dns1's rate-limit windows exactly, and each recovered ('UP' / 'retry ok') within a minute or two once the burst passed"], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "On gw.lan, exempt the internal zone from rebinding filtering in the unbound config generated by the firewall: add 'private-domain: \"lan.\"' (or otherwise exclude forwarded .lan answers from the private-address filter) so local names resolve for clients using gw as their second resolver. Secondarily, raise dns1.lan's ratelimit (currently 20 q/s per /24, too low for a whole LAN behind one source subnet) so normal bursts stop pushing clients over to the fallback path; consider raising the 10-second rewrite TTL on dns1 to reduce query volume.", "detail": "unbound private-address filtering suppresses forwarded .lan answers", "contributing": {"host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit"}}}
